# Server Environment Variables
# Copy this to .env and fill in the values

# Required
SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your-service-role-key

# Optional
PORT=3001
CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173,http://localhost:3000
RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000
RATE_LIMIT_MAX=100

# ─── Application base URL (auth emails) ──────────────────────────────────
# The dashboard's public https URL, used to build the links inside
# verification/password-reset emails (e.g. https://app.example.com/auth/
# reset-password?token=...). Prefer configuring this via the admin UI
# (Super Admin → Domains → app_base_url) instead — that takes priority over
# this env var when set. If neither is configured, the server falls back
# to a configured CORS_ORIGINS entry ONLY if it's a real absolute http(s)
# URL (never the CORS_ORIGINS='*' default) — and in production, if no
# valid base URL can be resolved at all, auth emails fail loudly instead
# of going out with a broken "*/auth/..." link.
# APP_BASE_URL=https://app.example.com

# ─── Client IP / proxy trust boundary ────────────────────────────────────
# Express `trust proxy` value. Default (unset) = "loopback, linklocal,
# uniquelocal", which is correct for Coolify / Traefik / nginx / Docker
# (the origin container is reached over a private bridge network).
# Never set this to `true`.
# TRUST_PROXY=loopback, linklocal, uniquelocal
#
# Extra peers allowed to set cf-connecting-ip / x-forwarded-for /
# cf-ipcountry. Only needed when the edge reaches the origin over a PUBLIC
# address. Comma-separated; exact IPs, or prefixes ending in "." / ":".
# TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS=203.0.113.7,172.70.

# ─── Widget asset manifest resolution ────────────────────────────────────
# In split deployments (Coolify multi-domain, separate Docker containers
# for frontend nginx + backend Express) the backend cannot read the
# frontend's filesystem. Set ONE of these so the backend can fetch the
# widget-manifest.json produced by `scripts/widget-hash.js`:
#
#   WIDGET_MANIFEST_URL  — explicit URL to widget-manifest.json
#   WIDGET_ASSET_BASE_URL — base URL where the frontend serves /widget/
#
# Without these the backend falls back to serving unhashed runtime.js with
# loaderVersion=unresolved (visible in /api/health/widget).
# WIDGET_MANIFEST_URL=https://example.com/widget/widget-manifest.json
# WIDGET_ASSET_BASE_URL=https://example.com

# Email — Resend (set if using Resend as email provider)
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_xxxxx

# Email — SendGrid (set if using SendGrid as email provider)
# SENDGRID_API_KEY=SG.xxxxx

# Email — SMTP (set if using SMTP as email provider)
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USER=your-user
# SMTP_PASS=your-password
# SMTP_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED=true

# ─── Realtime: Centrifugo ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Centrifugo runs as its own Coolify service (see docker-compose.centrifugo.yml
# and DEPLOY_CENTRIFUGO_COOLIFY.md). Connection details (ws_url, api_url,
# api_key, token_hmac_secret) are configured at runtime via:
#   Super Admin → Providers → Realtime
# and stored in the database, NOT in this .env. Do not duplicate them here.

# ── Phone verification (OTP) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Server-only pepper used to HMAC one-time codes and to fingerprint IPs for
# rate limiting. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# Without it the verification flow fails closed (no code is issued or checked).
PHONE_VERIFICATION_PEPPER=

# ── SEO — Google Search Console OAuth (optional) ────────────────────────────
# Required only for SEO → GSC Insights. Without all three set, that module
# reports "not configured" to the user rather than a raw OAuth error — see
# server/services/seo/gsc/oauthConfig.ts.
#
# 1. In Google Cloud Console, enable the "Google Search Console API".
# 2. OAuth consent screen: add scope .../auth/webmasters.readonly. While the
#    app is unverified/in Testing, only accounts added as Test users can
#    connect (max 100); Publishing for unrestricted use requires Google's
#    verification for this sensitive scope.
# 3. Credentials → Create Credentials → OAuth client ID → Web application.
#    Authorised redirect URIs must contain EXACTLY the value you put in
#    GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI below (byte-for-byte, no trailing slash) — and
#    that URL must be this BACKEND's own public origin (in a split Coolify
#    deployment, that's your api.* domain, not the frontend's app.* domain).
#    "Authorised JavaScript origins" is unused by this flow (server-side
#    auth-code exchange, no browser-side Google call) and may be left empty.
# 4. Also requires PLUGIN_SECRETS_MASTER_KEY (see the root .env.example —
#    it's a Core+Channels-wide secret, not SEO-specific) — the refresh
#    token is encrypted with it before being stored.
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=https://api.example.com/api/seo/gsc/oauth/callback

# ── Gmail channel plugin (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Required only to offer the Gmail plugin / Email Inbox. Reuses the SAME
# Google Cloud OAuth Client as GSC above (GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID/SECRET) —
# Google allows multiple redirect URIs per Client, so this only needs one
# more URI added to the Client you already created, not a second project.
# See server/services/channels/gmail/oauthConfig.ts and
# SELF_HOST_GUIDE.md's "Gmail channel plugin" section for the full setup
# (Pub/Sub topic + push subscription + publish-rights grant).
#
# 1. Enable the "Gmail API" on the same Google Cloud project used for GSC.
# 2. OAuth consent screen: also add scopes .../auth/gmail.modify and
#    .../auth/gmail.send (same consent screen as GSC — Testing-mode limits
#    and verification requirements apply the same way).
# 3. Credentials → the SAME OAuth client used for GSC → Authorised redirect
#    URIs → add the EXACT value of GOOGLE_GMAIL_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI below.
# 4. Cloud Pub/Sub → create a topic (e.g. `gmail-inbox-push`) → grant Publish
#    rights on it to the service account `gmail-api-push@system.gserviceaccount.com`
#    (this is Google's own fixed service account, not one you create) →
#    create a push subscription on that topic whose endpoint is
#    https://api.example.com/webhooks/gmail/push, with a push auth service
#    account and audience matching GMAIL_PUBSUB_PUSH_AUDIENCE below.
# GOOGLE_GMAIL_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=https://api.example.com/api/plugins/gmail/oauth/callback
GMAIL_PUBSUB_TOPIC=
# Must match the push subscription's configured OIDC audience — the
# convention Google's own docs use is the push endpoint URL itself.
# GMAIL_PUBSUB_PUSH_AUDIENCE=https://api.example.com/webhooks/gmail/push

# ── Yahoo Mail channel plugin (optional) ────────────────────────────────────
# Required only to offer the Yahoo Mail plugin / Email Inbox. Unlike Gmail,
# Yahoo has no existing platform OAuth Client to reuse — this is its own
# Yahoo Developer Network app, with its own three env vars. Feeds the SAME
# Email Inbox schema/UI as Gmail once connected.
#
# 1. Create an app at developer.yahoo.com/apps — API Permissions: "Mail"
#    (Read/Write). This is the only permission needed; it is what grants
#    IMAP/SMTP XOAUTH2 access, there is no separate "enable an API" step
#    the way Google requires.
# 2. Redirect URI(s): add the EXACT value of YAHOO_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI below.
# 3. Also requires PLUGIN_SECRETS_MASTER_KEY (see above) — the refresh
#    token is encrypted with it before being stored.
# 4. The Channels Worker (Dockerfile.worker) also needs
#    YAHOO_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID/SECRET/REDIRECT_URI — unlike Gmail's Worker-side
#    refresh, Yahoo's token endpoint requires redirect_uri on every refresh
#    call too, not just the initial code exchange.
YAHOO_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
YAHOO_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# YAHOO_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=https://api.example.com/api/plugins/yahoo/oauth/callback
# Optional — IMAP poll cadence in ms (default 75000 = 75s; no push webhook
# exists for third-party Yahoo Mail apps, so this is the only delivery path).
# CHANNELS_YAHOO_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=75000

# ── Platform admin bootstrap ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The ONLY account allowed to become the first platform admin
# (POST /api/admin-status/bootstrap). Must exactly match (case-insensitive)
# the verified email of the first-party account attempting to bootstrap.
# Unset by default — bootstrap is disabled until this is configured, so an
# arbitrary public signup can never win a first-user-wins race to become
# admin on a fresh, publicly reachable install.
# INITIAL_ADMIN_EMAIL=owner@example.com

# ── Self-host billing-less mode ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# This self-host chain ships with no billing/plans subsystem (no
# billing_plans/workspace_subscriptions tables, no check_workspace_entitlement
# RPC). Set to exactly `unlimited` ONLY if you have deliberately deployed
# without that subsystem and want workspace-level limits (e.g. max_agents) to
# be treated as unlimited instead of failing closed. Unset by default —
# without it, any limit check that cannot reach check_workspace_entitlement
# fails closed (503), matching hosted's behavior. Never set this for a
# deployment that has installed real billing/plans schema.
# SELF_HOST_BILLING_MODE=unlimited

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# REVERSIBLE "STOP DOING THIS" SWITCHES
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Six independent switches. EVERY ONE OF THEM DEFAULTS TO TODAY'S BEHAVIOUR:
# unset, empty, or ANY value other than the literal string `off` (compared
# after trim + lowercase) leaves the thing running. A typo therefore never
# silently turns something off. All are read once at boot, so changing one
# means restarting the process; each one that IS off logs a single line at
# startup naming what was skipped and how to restore it.
#
# Nothing below drops a table, changes a schema or removes an implementation.
# Turning any of them back on restores the behaviour exactly; only the rows
# that were not written during the off window are gone.

# ── 1. Observability reporting tickers ──────────────── RISK: safe ─────────
# Set to exactly `off` to skip the six OBSERVE-AND-REPORT tickers at boot:
# the alerting ticker (60s), the perf/process trend collectors (60s,
# in-memory only), the reliability+business hourly rollup (10 min), the
# auto-actions ticker (60s), the auto-actions cache (7s SELECT) and the
# SLO+enforcement ticker (60s). They observe the system and write history;
# on an install with little or no traffic they are pure database churn —
# ~2,880 ticker-lease writes/day, the alert_events churn, ~20k no-op
# slo_breach write transactions/day and ~12.3k cache SELECTs/day, for
# reports nobody opens.
#
# COST OF `off`: no new alert_events incidents and no outbound alert
# webhooks (the admin alert panel freezes at its last state); no new hourly
# buckets, so the reliability and business-KPI charts stop filling; the
# admin process-trend chart goes empty (live request metrics are NOT
# affected — the collector stays lazy and keeps feeding them); no
# slo_breach_events, so the admin breach list stays empty and any
# enforcement rule with an SLO trigger never fires — a real incident needs a
# manual admin auto-action instead of automatic self-throttling.
# NOTHING REFUSES WORK AND NO VISITOR SEES A CHANGE: auto-actions only ever
# REMOVE capability and the runtime check fails open, so with the producers
# stopped visitors keep full realtime transport, typing indicators, video
# and no throttling. One admin-UX cost: a manually activated auto-action
# lapses after ~60s instead of running its full TTL.
# NOT affected: realtime failover (its own switch, below), the call queue,
# billing, deletions, invitations and every other worker.
# The admin UI's own per-workspace alerting toggle becomes cosmetic — the
# env wins.
# OBSERVABILITY_REPORTING_TICKERS=off

# ── 2. Realtime failover engine ticker ──────────────── RISK: caution ──────
# Set to exactly `off` to skip the 30s realtime provider health probe and
# the failover/failback decision engine. This is the LARGEST periodic writer
# left once switch 1 is off: ~5,760 ticker-lease writes/day, roughly double
# the alerting lease that switch 1 stops. It has its own switch because it
# is NOT reporting — it decides which realtime provider live traffic uses.
#
# COST OF `off`: REALTIME KEEPS WORKING, PINNED. Every /connect and every
# handshake reads the persisted state directly (falling back to
# `centrifugo` if the row is missing), so the effective provider simply
# FREEZES at its last value — on a healthy install that IS the default and
# a visitor sees nothing. What stops: if the provider later dies, nothing
# automatically moves the platform to the polling fallback and nothing ever
# fails back. Chat then degrades per-connection instead of switching
# provider, and recovery means setting the realtime provider lock by hand
# in the admin panel (that path still works). No new provider-audit
# transition rows.
# REALTIME_FAILOVER_TICKER=off

# ── 3. Product-analytics logging ────────────────────── RISK: safe ─────────
# Set to exactly `off` to turn the charts-only telemetry writes into no-ops:
# visitor_page_views, web_analytics_events, widget_smart_events (the
# TypeScript half only — the AI-nudge lifecycle rows are written by a
# Postgres function and are out of reach of any env var),
# ai_agent_debug_events and ai_usage_logs. Nothing in the product reads any
# of them back to make a decision.
#
# SAVES NOTHING TODAY on an install with no visitors — these are all
# request-path writes, so at zero traffic they already cost zero. The value
# is that they STAY at zero when real traffic arrives; visitor_page_views is
# the fastest-growing table here once visitors exist.
# COST OF `off`: visitor journey and page-path reports plus the per-visitor
# page history in the Inbox go blank; the custom-events report has no data;
# smart-rule and AI-nudge conversion analytics go blank, so you cannot tell
# whether a proactive engagement rule works; the AI "answer inspected" debug
# trail disappears, making a wrong AI answer harder to reconstruct; the
# admin AI provider/model breakdown chart and the per-workspace usage panel
# lose their data source.
# NO billing, legal, routing or visitor-facing impact. AI BILLING IS
# UNAFFECTED: it reads ai_usage_events, written inside Postgres, which this
# switch does not touch. Operator-uploaded bot-visit imports are also
# deliberately excluded — silently dropping an explicit human-initiated
# import would look like a bug.
# PRODUCT_ANALYTICS_LOGGING=off

# ── 4. Delivery-diagnostics logging ─────────────────── RISK: caution ──────
# Set to exactly `off` to no-op the "did it actually send / did it actually
# sync" trails: email_logs, channel_delivery_attempts and
# ai_source_sync_logs. Separate from switch 3 because a human reads these
# AFTER something has already gone wrong.
#
# SAVES NOTHING TODAY (all request-path writes, zero at zero traffic).
# COST OF `off`: you lose the only evidence that a transactional email —
# invitation, password reset, invoice — was actually sent, which is the
# first thing anyone checks when a customer says "I never got the email";
# the workspace-integration and admin email panels go blank and the GDPR
# subject-access export's email section becomes empty; a failed WhatsApp or
# Telegram send loses its per-attempt error code, latency and attempt number
# (you still see the final job status); and the Data Hub knowledge-source
# screen can no longer say whether the last sync succeeded and shows no sync
# history — the one user-visible regression here, and the reason this is
# `caution` rather than `safe`.
# SENDING, RETRY AND INGESTION THEMSELVES ARE UNAFFECTED: retry state lives
# on channel_jobs, and source status lives on ai_data_sources.
# DELIVERY_DIAGNOSTICS_LOGGING=off

# ── 5. Compliance audit logging ─── RISK: do_not_disable_in_production ─────
# Set to exactly `off` to no-op the financial, security and legal record
# written from TypeScript: audit_logs, security_events, login_attempts,
# admin_gate_bypass_log, plan_change_log, commerce_tool_audit and
# realtime_provider_audit. This switch exists so the decision is yours and
# is written down — not because turning it off is advisable.
#
# SAVES NOTHING. Every one of these tables is request-path-only and writes
# nothing at all on an idle install, so there is no database churn to weigh
# against the following.
# COST OF `off`: the legal record of administrative and privacy actions, and
# the GDPR subject-access export loses its audit_logs section, which it is
# required to include. The only forensic trail if this publicly reachable
# install is probed or compromised — the admin security panel goes blank and
# nothing can answer "what happened". Account-takeover evidence, plus the
# user-visible "recent login history" in Account settings (note: login
# attempts do NOT drive lockout, so the loss is forensic, not enforcement —
# brute-force throttling is in-memory and keeps working). Any trace at all
# of an admin gate bypass, the highest-privilege escape hatch in the
# product, so privilege escalation becomes untraceable. Billing-dispute
# evidence of what plan a customer was on and when it changed. The record of
# AI actions taken against a merchant's store, and of realtime transport
# config changes.
# OUT OF REACH of this or any env var (stopping them needs a migration):
# billing_v2_audit, ai_billing_audit_log, verification_attempts,
# verification_delivery_attempts and the storage_usage_logs quota trigger —
# all written by PERFORM inside Postgres functions.
# COMPLIANCE_AUDIT_LOGGING=off

# ── 6. Channels worker liveness heartbeat ───────────── RISK: caution ──────
# Set to exactly `off` to stop the channels worker's 45s liveness upsert to
# channel_worker_heartbeats (~1,920 writes/day against a single row — one of
# the largest cumulative writers in the database, and NOT reachable by
# switch 1, because the writer is the channels worker itself).
# SET THE SAME VALUE ON BOTH CORE AND THE CHANNELS WORKER: Core reads it to
# relax the consumer gate, the worker reads it to stop writing.
#
# TRY THE CHEAPER LEVER FIRST: raising CHANNELS_HEARTBEAT_MS drops ~85% of
# these writes with no code change and no loss of function — but only up to
# about 100s, because the staleness windows are 120s (the provider-operation
# gate) and 150s (the admin health panel). Past that the gate starts
# flapping. The cadence was already raised from 15s to 45s for this reason.
#
# COST OF `off`: the Super Admin channels-health panel reports the worker
# offline or unknown forever even while it is alive and draining the queue
# normally. Because Core relaxes the gate under the same flag, Telegram
# diagnostics and webhook repair no longer fail fast with a clean 503 when
# the worker really IS dead — the operator waits out the 12-15s operation
# timeout and gets a worse error message instead. That is a worse message,
# not refused work: turning this off never makes a request path start
# refusing.
# CHANNEL MESSAGING IS COMPLETELY UNAFFECTED — the claim loop, the delivery
# pipeline and channel_jobs never read this table.
# CHANNELS_WORKER_HEARTBEAT=off

# ── Workspace invitations v5.1 ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Independent server-only secrets. Generate each separately with
# `openssl rand -hex 32`; never reuse either value for sessions or providers.
INVITATION_LINK_SECRET=
INVITATION_OTP_PEPPER=
# Optional rotation ring, each value at least 32 bytes. Keep old versions
# until every email job/token derived with them has expired.
# INVITATION_LINK_SECRET_RING={"1":"...","2":"..."}
# INVITATION_LINK_KEY_VERSION=2
# Dedicated production worker: WORKER_KIND=invitations
# Local/dev-only in-process worker: INVITATION_WORKER_INPROC=1

# ── Generic Verification Core v1 (dormant — no consumer enabled) ───────────
# server/services/verification/ — a domain-neutral OTP/proof engine for
# FUTURE use (signup verification, password reset, step-up, etc). Every
# purpose ships disabled by default; no route or worker calls into this
# subsystem yet, so these values are not required for any current feature.
# Independent server-only secret. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# Never reuse this value for sessions, invitations, or phone verification.
#
# Malformed or internally inconsistent configuration FAILS STARTUP with a
# dedicated VerificationConfigError rather than being silently ignored:
# invalid JSON in the ring, a non-object ring, an invalid version key, a
# ring value shorter than 32 characters, an explicitly empty ring object,
# GENERIC_VERIFICATION_PEPPER disagreeing with ring version "1", or a
# GENERIC_VERIFICATION_KEY_VERSION that is invalid or absent from the ring
# — see server/services/verification/crypto.ts's loadRing/currentVersion.
GENERIC_VERIFICATION_PEPPER=
# Optional rotation ring, each value at least 32 bytes, keyed by version
# number as a JSON object (e.g. {"1":"...","2":"..."}). If BOTH
# GENERIC_VERIFICATION_PEPPER and ring version "1" are set, they must be
# byte-identical — the same secret occupying the same slot, never two
# independent values where one silently wins.
#
# IMPORTANT — version 1 is not an ordinary rotation slot. It also backs the
# permanently-pinned STABLE_INDEX_KEY_VERSION (destination hash, subject-ref
# hash, IP rate-limit hash, idempotency key, request fingerprint — see
# crypto.ts's "TWO SEPARATE ROTATION DOMAINS" comment). "Keep old versions
# until every challenge derived with them has expired" (correct and
# sufficient for the ORDINARY OTP/proof rotation pointer, below) is NOT
# sufficient for version 1: as long as ANY live challenge, proof, rate-limit
# window, or idempotency ledger row was indexed under it, removing it
# breaks every lookup against those rows, not just OTP verification.
#
# There is also no migration that can silently "recompute" those hashes:
# they are HMACs over the ORIGINAL raw input (the actual email/phone, the
# actual IP, the actual caller-supplied request id) — this codebase does
# not retain those raw inputs anywhere once hashed (by design — see
# docs/GENERIC_VERIFICATION_SECURITY.md), so nothing can rehash them under
# a new version after the fact. The only supported procedure to rotate the
# STABLE INDEX version itself is:
#   1. Quiesce verification traffic for this subsystem.
#   2. Wait for every existing challenge, proof, rate-limit window, and
#      idempotency ledger entry to expire under its own TTL.
#   3. Purge the expired rows (gv_purge_expired_idempotency, plus the
#      normal challenge/proof TTL cleanup).
#   4. Only THEN change STABLE_INDEX_KEY_VERSION in crypto.ts (a source
#      change, not an env var) and deploy.
#   5. Resume traffic.
# The alternative is a genuinely version-aware dual-read migration (check
# both the old- and new-version hash until every row is re-hashed) — this
# codebase does not implement one; the procedure above is what's supported.
#
# The ORDINARY OTP/proof rotation pointer below is unaffected by any of
# this and rotates freely: keep the old version in the ring until every
# challenge/proof created under it has expired, then remove it.
# GENERIC_VERIFICATION_PEPPER_RING={"1":"...","2":"..."}
# GENERIC_VERIFICATION_KEY_VERSION=2
# Plan-authoritative self-host installs must set an integer seat limit.
# SELF_HOST_SEAT_LIMIT=25
